"""Author the late GY1979 records. Research/correction evidence is logged separately.
All mathematical formulations labelled reconstruction are editorial, not a transcription.
Run from any directory; writes only the sibling part files.
"""
from pathlib import Path
import json,csv,collections
P=Path(__file__).resolve().parent
T=[]; I=[]; Q=[]; C=[]
def save():
    for name,data in [('talks',T),('items',I),('problems',Q)]:
        (P/(name+'.json')).write_text(json.dumps(data,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2)+'\n')
    with (P/'corrections.csv').open('w',newline='') as f:
        w=csv.DictWriter(f,fieldnames=['page','place','transcription_reads','should_read','evidence','applies_to','type']);w.writeheader();w.writerows(C)
def talk(r,date,speaker,pages,title,summary,kind='talk'):
    T.append(dict(record=r,date=date,speaker=speaker,pages=pages,title=title,kind=kind,summary=summary));save()
def item(r,pages,kind,title,notes,statement,reconstruction,cl,fate,status,refs,lc='correct with missing hypotheses',pm='exact',v='read the result',dev='annotated',problem='',worth='',conf='high',speaker=None):
    n=1+sum(x['record']==r for x in I)
    x=dict(id=f'1979-{r}.{n}',record=r,pages=pages,kind=kind,title=title,notes=notes,statement=statement,reconstruction=reconstruction,**{'class':cl},fate=fate,present_status='As of 10 November 2026: '+status,literal_correctness=lc,publication_match=pm,verification=v,development=dev,problem=problem,worth=worth,refs=refs,confidence=conf)
    if speaker:x['speaker']=speaker
    I.append(x);save();return x['id']
def corr(page,place,reads,should,evidence,typ='faithful_error',applies='both'):
    C.append(dict(page=page,place=place,transcription_reads=reads,should_read=should,evidence=evidence,applies_to=applies,type=typ));save()

talk(481,'','-','P000900-P000901','The catalog heading YEAR 1980',r'This undated catalog record is a year divider, not a separate mathematical session. The supplied page window overlaps Migdal: the first visible heading on P000900 is explicitly 21 January 1980. No mathematical item is duplicated under this heading.',kind='heading')

talk(482,'1980-01-21','A. A. Migdal','P000900-P000905','Yang--Mills theory in loop space',r'The actual notes occupy P000900--P000904; P000905 starts the next session. Migdal replaces a gauge potential by traces of parallel transport, defines area and path derivatives, and converts the classical Yang--Mills equation and formal Schwinger--Dyson identity to loop equations. Color contraction produces a multiloop hierarchy; normalized large-rank factorization formally closes it. The final assertion about confinement is a research aspiration, not a proof recorded in these pages. Gelfand intervenes on characters of the path group. The principal equation had already appeared in December 1979; the talk is not its first publication.')
item(482,'P000900','construction','Gauge connections, ordered parallel transport and Wilson loops',r'The notes write \(A_\mu=\sum_aT_aA_\mu^a\), \(\Psi(C)=\operatorname{Tr}P\exp\oint_C A\,dx\), and describe cancellation of an outward path followed by its reverse.',r'For a smooth matrix connection and a piecewise smooth parametrized path \(\gamma\), parallel transport is the unique solution of the associated first-order matrix ODE, with prescribed initial identity. Reversing \(\gamma\) inverts transport, and concatenation multiplies transports in the chosen order. For a loop based at \(x\), gauge transformation conjugates \(U(C)\) by the gauge matrix at \(x\), so \(\operatorname{Tr}U(C)\) is gauge invariant.',r'The derivative \(dx\) in (3) is interpreted as \(dx^\mu\) contracted with \(A_\mu\). The side of matrix multiplication, sign of the connection, coupling and factors of \(i\) require a convention; the notes do not fix all of them.','E',r'Wilson-loop gauge variables predate this talk: \citet[Secs.~II--III]{wilson1974confinement}; the corresponding loop notation is explicit in \citet[Sec.~4.1]{makeenko2000large}.','A standard construction, with a fixed connection/transport sign convention. It is not by itself a construction of a continuum quantum measure.',['wilson1974confinement','makeenko2000large'])
item(482,'P000900','claim','Vanishing of gauge-variant expectations needs a precise observable',r'\ru{Неинвар. величины в квант. теории получаются равными 0}: noninvariant quantities have zero quantum expectation.',r'For a gauge-invariant finite-lattice measure without gauge fixing, the expectation of an observable equals that of its average over local gauge transformations. Hence an observable whose gauge average vanishes has zero expectation. A general noninvariant observable need not have zero expectation: it may contain a nonzero invariant component.',r'The gauge-average qualification is necessary. The notebook sentence is false if read as quantifying over every noninvariant function.','E',r'\citet[abstract and gauge-invariance argument]{elitzur1975local} predates the seminar. The finite-volume projection statement also follows immediately by a change of variables in the invariant measure.','The corrected projection statement is established; gauge fixing changes the assertion about gauge-dependent quantities.',['elitzur1975local'],lc='false as written',v='publisher metadata checked')
item(482,'P000900-P000902','remark','Gelfand on path-group characters and connections',r'Gelfand says that a representation of the group of paths is determined by its character, and later calls such a representation a connection; curvature is compared with a Lie algebra.',r'Finite-dimensional unitary representations of a group with identical characters are equivalent: restrict to the compact closure of their joint image and use compact-group character theory. A smooth connection determines a smooth transport functor on the thin path groupoid. Conversely, reconstructing a connection requires regularity and compatibility of transport, not merely an arbitrary algebraic representation of paths.',r'The finite-dimensional/unitary hypothesis and thin-path smoothness distinguish two claims compressed in the intervention. The curvature analogy is not a literal identification of curvature with a Lie algebra.','E',r'Holonomy reconstruction is developed precisely in \citet[abstract]{barrett1991holonomy}. This later theorem clarifies the missing hypotheses; it is a partial match to Gelfand\textquotesingle s informal character argument, not evidence of a new 1980 theorem.','Established after specifying the category and regularity. The notes contain no unrestricted reconstruction theorem.',['barrett1991holonomy'],pm='partial',v='publisher metadata checked',speaker='I. M. Gelfand',conf='medium')
item(482,'P000900-P000901','definition','Stokes-type loop functionals and the area derivative',r'A small attached loop defines \(\delta\Psi/\delta\sigma_{\mu\nu}\); the notes explicitly warn that length is not a functional of this kind. Formula (7)/(10) inserts \(F_{\mu\nu}(x)\) inside the ordered trace.',r'For smooth transport and an infinitesimal oriented rectangle at a marked point \(x\), the first term in its area is the curvature insertion: \(\delta W(C)/\delta\sigma^{\mu\nu}(x)=N^{-1}\operatorname{Tr}(F_{\mu\nu}(x)U(C_x))\), up to the agreed connection sign. A Stokes functional is insensitive to retracing and has an area derivative with this scaling; perimeter is not such a functional at a newly attached small loop.',r'Normalized \(W=N^{-1}\Psi\) is introduced for later consistency; the notes use unnormalized \(\Psi\). Orientation and the placement of the marked insertion must be fixed.','E',r'The exact differential identity appears in \citet[Eq.~(147)]{makeenko2000large}, explicitly identified there as the Mandelstam formula.','A classical differential identity, not a license to differentiate an unrenormalized quantum Wilson loop without a regulator.',['makeenko2000large'])
item(482,'P000901','method','Curvature from an infinitesimal commutator',r'Formula (13) writes a product of four factors \(1\pm dx_\mu\nabla_\mu\) as \(1+dx_\mu\wedge dx_\nu[\nabla_\mu,\nabla_\nu]\); (11)--(14) implement shifts by differential operators.',r'For two smooth infinitesimal transport directions, \(e^{aD_\mu}e^{bD_\nu}e^{-aD_\mu}e^{-bD_\nu}=1+ab[D_\mu,D_\nu]+O(|a|^2|b|+|a||b|^2)\). The mixed \(ab\) coefficient gives curvature. Replacing inverse exponentials by \(1-aD_\mu\) introduces unwanted pure quadratic terms unless only the mixed coefficient is retained.',r'Equation (13) must be read as a mixed infinitesimal identity, not as equality of finite products.','E',r'This is the elementary small-rectangle proof underlying \citet[Eq.~(147)]{makeenko2000large}.','Valid as a mixed derivative or a properly truncated holonomy expansion. A literal finite-product identity is false.',['makeenko2000large'],lc='false as written')
item(482,'P000901-P000902','construction','Marked path derivatives become covariant derivatives',r'Formulas (15)--(18) move the marked point, add a short connector and shift both ends of a closed transport; the notes emphasize that the left-hand derivative is noncommutative.',r'For a curvature-inserted loop \(\phi_{\mu\nu}(C_x)=\operatorname{Tr}(F_{\mu\nu}(x)U(C_x))\), the derivative defined by translating the insertion with short parallel-transport connectors is \(\partial^x_\alpha\phi_{\mu\nu}=\operatorname{Tr}((D_\alpha F_{\mu\nu})(x)U(C_x))\). Iteration gives covariant derivatives in the specified order. This operator is not an ordinary partial derivative on a scalar function of \(x\) alone.',r'We make explicit the connector-based definition that the notes state after using it. This resolves their warning about noncommutativity.','E',r'\citet[Eqs.~(148)--(150)]{makeenko2000large} gives the precise path-derivative dictionary.','Established for classical smooth connections and suitable loop functionals.',['makeenko2000large'])
item(482,'P000902','claim','Translation of a contour and the area-derivative integral',r'Formula (19) decomposes whole-contour translation into endpoint derivatives and an integral of area derivatives; (20) specializes to translation-invariant closed-contour functionals.',r'For an open-path Stokes functional with differentiable endpoint and shape variations, a uniform translation is the sum of its endpoint variations and the swept-strip area variation. With antisymmetric area convention this has the form \(\partial_{C,\mu}=\partial_{x,\mu}+\partial_{y,\mu}+\int_C dz^\alpha\,\delta/\delta\sigma^{\alpha\mu}(z)\). Vanishing of the left side gives the identity used for translation-invariant functionals, with endpoint conventions retained.',r'This is a variational identity on a specified domain, not an operator identity for arbitrary functions on curves. The sign in (20) uses antisymmetry of the two-form index.','E',r'The relevant area/path-derivative calculus is recorded in \citet[Sec.~4.3]{makeenko2000large}. The exact notebook notation in (19) is a partial match.','A conditional shape-variation formula; no independent open problem is stated.',['makeenko2000large'],pm='partial',conf='medium')
item(482,'P000900-P000903','theorem','The classical Yang--Mills equation in loop notation',r'From \(D_\mu F_{\mu\nu}=0\), formulas (15), (15\textquotesingle) and (24) obtain \(\partial^x_\mu\delta\Psi/\delta\sigma_{\mu\nu}(x)=0\). Formulas (21)--(23) invoke the curvature-squared action.',r'For a smooth solution of the source-free Yang--Mills equation on a Euclidean domain, the marked path derivative of the curvature-inserted Wilson loop vanishes: \(\partial^x_\mu\delta W(C)/\delta\sigma_{\mu\nu}(x)=N^{-1}\operatorname{Tr}((D_\mu F_{\mu\nu})U(C_x))=0\). With \(S=\beta\int\operatorname{Tr}F_{\mu\nu}F_{\mu\nu}\) summed over ordered indices and trace-pairing convention, variation gives \(\delta S/\delta A_\nu=-4\beta D_\mu F_{\mu\nu}\); alternate action normalization changes the prefactor.',r'The notebook (23) has mismatched free indices and suppresses the action-dependent prefactor and sign. Only the zero set is unaffected by an overall nonzero factor.','E',r'\citet[Sec.~4.3, especially the vocabulary following Eq.~(150)]{makeenko2000large} gives the classical correspondence.','Established. It does not identify the quantum large-rank equation with the classical Yang--Mills equation.',['makeenko2000large'])
item(482,'P000903','method','Normalized expectation and the weak Schwinger--Dyson identity',r'(25) displays adjacent integrals for the expectation; (26)--(28) integrate a functional derivative and replace the classical equation by \(\hbar\delta/\delta A\).',r'For a finite-dimensional regulator with density \(Z^{-1}e^{-S(a)/\hbar}\,da\), integrable \(Q\) and vanishing boundary terms, \(\langle(\partial_jS)Q\rangle=\hbar\langle\partial_jQ\rangle\). The continuum expression is formal until a regulator, measure, gauge treatment and renormalization are specified. The equality with a differential operator holds inside expectations, not as equality of ordinary functions.',r'The partition function in (25) must divide the numerator. The required decay/domain assumptions are not in the notebook.','E',r'\citet[Eqs.~(135)--(136)]{makeenko2000large} explicitly calls the operator replacement a weak identity.','A rigorous integration-by-parts identity for appropriate regulators, and a formal continuum identity unless independently justified.',['makeenko2000large'],lc='false as written')
item(482,'P000904','theorem','The color contraction is specific to the defining unitary representation',r'(31)--(33) split the transport at an insertion and claim a two-trace contraction for an arbitrary semisimple group, with the correction \(-N^{-1}\operatorname{Tr}(UV)\).',r'For traceless Hermitian generators of \(\mathfrak{su}(N)\) normalized by \(\operatorname{Tr}(T^aT^b)=\delta^{ab}\), completeness gives \(\sum_a(T^a)_{ij}(T^a)_{kl}=\delta_{il}\delta_{jk}-N^{-1}\delta_{ij}\delta_{kl}\). Consequently \(\sum_a\operatorname{Tr}(T^aUT^aV)=\operatorname{Tr}U\operatorname{Tr}V-N^{-1}\operatorname{Tr}(UV)\). Adding the normalized identity generator gives the \(U(N)\) version without the subtraction.',r'The group, defining representation, and trace normalization are essential. The formula is not valid unchanged for every semisimple group.','E',r'This is the color identity used in \citet[Sec.~4.2 and Eq.~(157)]{makeenko2000large}.','An exact finite-dimensional identity in the stated convention. The notebook\textquotesingle s unrestricted group quantifier is false.',['makeenko2000large'],lc='false as written')
item(482,'P000903-P000904','theorem','Finite-rank loop equations form a hierarchy',r'Equation (34) relates the derivative of one loop expectation to a two-loop expectation. The notes correctly say that one loop is insufficient and include self-intersections.',r'Writing \(W(C)=N^{-1}\langle\operatorname{Tr}U(C)\rangle\), the formal \(SU(N)\) equation in a conventional coupling normalization is \[\partial^x_\mu\frac{\delta W(C)}{\delta\sigma_{\mu\nu}(x)}=\lambda\oint_Cdy_\nu\,\delta^{(d)}(x-y)\left(W_2(C_{xy},C_{yx})-\frac{1}{N^2}W(C)\right).\] Here \(W_2=\langle N^{-1}\operatorname{Tr}U(C_{xy})\,N^{-1}\operatorname{Tr}U(C_{yx})\rangle\). Higher products generate a splitting/joining hierarchy.',r'The \(SU(N)\) subtraction, trace normalization and coupling are restored. Contact terms and coincident points require a regulator.','E',r'\citet[pp.~135--137]{makeenkomigdal1979exact} was published 3 December 1979, before this talk; \citet[Eqs.~(157), (161)]{makeenko2000large} supplies the explicit finite-rank forms.','The formal hierarchy is established; rigorous results are available in specified regulated and lower-dimensional settings, not an unrestricted four-dimensional continuum theorem.',['makeenkomigdal1979exact','makeenko2000large'])
item(482,'P000904','method','Gauge-covariant regularization must reconnect separated endpoints',r'The notes warn that simply smoothing \(\delta(x-y)\) opens the two contours; smoothing must be accompanied by closing them.',r'When \(x\ne y\) in a point-split color contraction, open transports transform at different endpoints. To preserve gauge invariance one must include connecting parallel transports (and specify their paths), or use a gauge-invariant lattice regulator. A scalar mollifier alone cannot turn the separated open factors into gauge-invariant Wilson loops.',r'We spell out the endpoint transformation argument. The notes give no complete regularization prescription or convergence theorem.','E',r'The loop-space regularization programme is explicit in \citet[Sec.~4.6]{makeenko2000large}; it develops the same gauge-covariance requirement.','A valid methodological constraint; existence of a continuum limit is a separate problem.',['makeenko2000large'],pm='partial')
item(482,'P000904','claim','Large-rank factorization requires normalized traces',r'(35) states \(\langle\Psi(C_1)\cdots\Psi(C_m)\rangle=\prod_i\langle\Psi(C_i)\rangle+O(N^{-2})\), then observes \(\Psi(0)=N\) and redefines \(\Psi=N\bar\Phi\).',r'For fixed loops, normalized single-trace observables \(\Phi=N^{-1}\operatorname{Tr}U\), fixed \(\lambda=g^2N\), and a regime admitting the usual topological expansion, \(\langle\Phi_1\cdots\Phi_m\rangle-\prod_i\langle\Phi_i\rangle=O(N^{-2})\). For the unnormalized \(\Psi_i\), the corresponding absolute error is \(O(N^{m-2})\). Factorization is an additional dynamical large-rank assertion, not a consequence of gauge invariance alone.',r'The order estimate in (35) is moved to the normalized variables introduced immediately below it. The topological-expansion regime is explicit.','E',r'\citet[Sec.~3.4 and Eqs.~(158)--(160)]{makeenko2000large} uses normalized observables and displays the closed large-rank equation.','Standard in perturbation theory and specified lattice regimes; the literal normalization in (35) is inconsistent.',['makeenko2000large'],lc='false as written')
item(482,'P000903-P000904','claim','The general confinement goal and the proposed large-rank route',r'The notes say the \(SU(\infty)\) theory is near an exact solution and conclude that the equation for \(\bar\Phi\) gives \ru{confinement}. No solution, positivity criterion, uniqueness argument, continuum limit or area-law proof is supplied.',r'A definite modern version asks for the physically selected, renormalized four-dimensional pure \(SU(N)\) Yang--Mills Wilson-loop expectations in the large-\(N\) limit, satisfying the loop equation, positivity and normalization, and a positive string tension in the large rectangular-loop limit. Closure of an equation alone neither constructs that solution nor proves its area law.',r'The notebook\textquotesingle s broad assertion is not a fully formulated theorem. The explicit pure-gauge, dimension, limit and positivity conditions define the identifiable confinement programme, rather than claiming those words were all spoken.','C',r'\citet[abstract]{wilson1974confinement} proves a strong-coupling lattice mechanism; \citet[main theorem]{chatterjee2015string} proves a rigorous large-rank lattice/string correspondence for \(SO(N)\) at strong coupling. These do not settle the continuum assertion. The continuing four-dimensional construction problem is described in \citet[problem statement]{jaffewitten2000yangmills}.','Partly developed. Controlled lattice and two-dimensional results do not constitute a general solution of four-dimensional continuum confinement from the loop equations.',['wilson1974confinement','chatterjee2015string','jaffewitten2000yangmills'],lc='not determinable',pm='partial',v='read the result',problem='GP-1975-10',worth='A sharply regulated special case can be worthwhile, but the unrestricted continuum confinement programme is not a plausible bounded contribution for this chapter.',conf='medium')
Q.append(dict(id='GP-1975-10',title='Confinement in four-dimensional Yang--Mills theory',statement=r'Give a mathematically controlled derivation of confinement for four-dimensional pure nonabelian Yang--Mills theory, for example a positive string tension for its physical continuum Wilson loops. This is the general goal already indexed as GP-1975-10. The normalized large-rank loop-equation method in 1979-482.13 is a proposed route, not an equivalent formulation or proof of the fixed-rank continuum assertion.',occurrences=['1975-377.10','1979-482.14'],status=r'As of 10 November 2026, rigorous strong-coupling lattice results such as \citet{chatterjee2015string} are partial. They do not establish the unrestricted four-dimensional continuum assertion; see \citet{jaffewitten2000yangmills}. The old id is reused only for the explicit general confinement goal; it does not identify the large-rank method with the earlier formulation.',status_date='2026-11',refs=['chatterjee2015string','jaffewitten2000yangmills']));save()

corr('P000900','Noninvariant expectations','All noninvariant quantities have zero expectation.','Only observables with zero gauge average must have zero expectation.','The scan and both transcriptions agree; an observable can have both invariant and noninvariant components. This is a mathematical qualification, not a paleographic correction.')
corr('P000901','Equation (13)',r'\((1+aD_\mu)(1+bD_\nu)(1-aD_\mu)(1-bD_\nu)=1+ab[D_\mu,D_\nu]\).',r'Read only the mixed infinitesimal coefficient, or use inverse exponential transports with remainder terms.','The scan confirms the four linear factors. Direct expansion leaves pure quadratic terms, so finite equality is false.')
corr('P000903','Equation (23)',r'\(\delta S/\delta A_\mu=\beta D_\mu F_{\mu\nu}\).',r'The variational denominator must have free index \(\nu\); the overall coefficient depends on the action and trace convention.','The scan explicitly shows the inconsistent free index. Varying the displayed action gives a nonzero scalar multiple of the Yang--Mills equation; it does not give the displayed exact equality.')
corr('P000903','Equation (25)',r'The numerator path integral is multiplied by the partition function.',r'Use the numerator divided by \(Z=\int\mathcal DA\,e^{-S}\).','The scan has two adjacent parenthesized factors and no visible inverse; normalization requires the second factor to be inverted. Faithful notebook omission.')
corr('P000903','Equation (28)',r'\(\delta S/\delta A=\hbar\delta/\delta A\).',r'An equality inside expectations after integration by parts, not an equality of functions and operators.','The preceding notebook equation (27) is the valid weak formulation; the displayed operator shorthand suppresses this domain.')
corr('P000904','Equation (33), group qualification','For an arbitrary semisimple group.',r'The displayed completeness formula is for \(\mathfrak{su}(N)\) in its defining representation, with specified generator normalization; \(U(N)\) has a related formula.','The handwritten Russian confirms the broad group qualification. Other representations and groups have different invariant contractions.')
corr('P000904','Equation (35)',r'Factorization error \(O(N^{-2})\) for \(\Psi=\operatorname{Tr}U\).',r'The error \(O(N^{-2})\) belongs to \(\bar\Phi=N^{-1}\Psi\); for a product of \(m\) unnormalized traces it scales as \(O(N^{m-2})\).',r'The scan immediately below (35) writes \(\Psi(0)=N\) and proposes the normalization. The mismatch is in the notebook itself.')

talk(483,'1980-01-28','A. V. Zelevinsky','P000905-P000906','Kazhdan--Lusztig polynomials and representations',r'The notes actually continue through the upper part of P000907, before MacPherson begins. Zelevinsky explicitly discusses the 1979 paper of Kazhdan and Lusztig: Coxeter and Hecke algebra preliminaries, the bar-invariant basis, multiplication and cell representations. The Verma-module multiplicity formula and Vogan\textquotesingle s cohomological reformulations are distinguished as conjectures. Their later proofs must not be confused with the basis theorem already proved in 1979.')
item(483,'P000905','definition','Coxeter presentations, reduced words and Bruhat order',r'Equations (1)--(3) define a Coxeter group and reduced words, and define the elements \(<w\) by deleting any subset of a reduced expression. Examples are symmetric and Weyl groups; the symmetric-group discussion displays only the neighboring braid relation.',r'Let \((W,S)\) be a Coxeter system with finite \(S\), exponents \(m(s,s)=1\), and \(m(s,t)\in\{2,3,\ldots,\infty\}\) for \(s\ne t\); no relation is imposed for exponent \(\infty\). The length is the least number of generators. The Bruhat relation \(y\le w\) holds exactly when a reduced expression for \(w\) has a subword whose product is \(y\); this does not depend on the chosen reduced expression. In \(S_n\), also require \(s_i s_j=s_j s_i\) for \(|i-j|>1\).',r'The empty deletion gives \(y=w\), so the general relation is \(\le\), not strict \(<\). The omitted distant commutations are needed for the symmetric-group presentation.','E',r'The talk\textquotesingle s named source is \citet[Sec.~1]{kazhdanlusztig1979representations}, received March and published June 1979.','Standard Coxeter theory; the two qualifications prevent literal misreadings of the compressed examples.',['kazhdanlusztig1979representations'],lc='false as written',v='publisher metadata checked')
item(483,'P000905-P000906','construction','The Hecke algebra, specialization and its standard basis',r'Equations (4)--(6) impose \((T_s+1)(T_s-q)=0\) and braid relations, note specialization \(q=1\), identify the finite-field double-coset algebra, and define \(T_w\) using a reduced word.',r'Over \(A=\mathbb Z[q^{1/2},q^{-1/2}]\), the equal-parameter Hecke algebra \(\mathcal H(W)\) has basis \(T_w\), with products along reduced expressions independent of the expression. Under \(q^{1/2}\mapsto1\) it becomes \(\mathbb Z[W]\). For a split finite reductive group over \(\mathbb F_q\) with Borel subgroup \(B\), the complex specialization is the algebra of \(B\)-bi-invariant functions, with convolution normalized so \(1_B\) is the identity and the double-coset generators satisfy the stated quadratic relation.',r'The tensor product with \(\mathbb C\) needs the specialization homomorphism. Splitness, equal parameters and convolution normalization are not written out in the notes.','E',r'\citet[Sec.~1]{kazhdanlusztig1979representations} is the explicit pre-talk source.','Established. Unequal-parameter or twisted finite groups require a modified statement.',['kazhdanlusztig1979representations'],v='publisher metadata checked')
item(483,'P000906','definition','Bar involution and inversion of Hecke generators',r'(7)--(9) prescribe \(\overline{q^{1/2}}=q^{-1/2}\), \(\overline{T_w}=T_{w^{-1}}^{-1}\), and \(T_s^{-1}=q^{-1}(T_s+1-q)\).',r'There is a semilinear algebra involution of \(\mathcal H\), semilinear over the involution \(q^{1/2}\mapsto q^{-1/2}\), determined by \(\overline{T_s}=T_s^{-1}\). The quadratic relation implies the displayed inverse, and the braid relations ensure well-definedness. Thus \(\overline{T_w}=T_{w^{-1}}^{-1}\).',r'It is semilinear, not \(A\)-linear; the inverse in the subscript formula is distinguished from inversion of \(w\).','E',r'\citet[Sec.~1]{kazhdanlusztig1979representations}; the generator calculation is supplied in the notebook itself.','An exact algebraic construction.',['kazhdanlusztig1979representations'],lc='correct',v='publisher metadata checked')
item(483,'P000906','theorem','Existence and uniqueness of the Kazhdan--Lusztig basis',r'\ru{Теорема 1.1}: a unique \(C^{\prime}_w\) is bar invariant and has expansion \(q^{-\ell(w)/2}\sum_{y\le w}P_{y,w}T_y\), with \(P_{w,w}=1\) and the strict degree bound (12).',r'For every Coxeter system and every \(w\in W\), there is a unique \(C^{\prime}_w=q^{-\ell(w)/2}\sum_{y\le w}P_{y,w}(q)T_y\) such that \(\overline{C^{\prime}_w}=C^{\prime}_w\), \(P_{w,w}=1\), \(P_{y,w}\in\mathbb Z[q]\), and \(\deg P_{y,w}\le(\ell(w)-\ell(y)-1)/2\) for \(y<w\). These elements form an \(A\)-basis.',r'For \(y\not\le w\), set \(P_{y,w}=0\). The bound is interpreted integrally and the zero polynomial is allowed.','E',r'The source and theorem number match \citet[Thm.~1.1]{kazhdanlusztig1979representations}, published in 1979.','Established for arbitrary Coxeter systems. This algebraic existence theorem is separate from geometric positivity or character formulas.',['kazhdanlusztig1979representations'],lc='correct',v='publisher metadata checked')
item(483,'P000906','construction','The simple-reflection polynomial and the second canonical basis',r'The calculation gives \(C^{\prime}_s=q^{-1/2}(T_s+1)\), \(P_{e,s}=P_{s,s}=1\); (16) defines \(C_w=\varepsilon_wj(C^{\prime}_w)\).',r'The semilinear involution \(j\) is specified by \(j(q^{1/2})=q^{-1/2}\) and \(j(T_w)=(-1)^{\ell(w)}q^{-\ell(w)}T_w\). It gives the alternative basis \(C_w=(-1)^{\ell(w)}j(C^{\prime}_w)\). In rank one, \(C_s=q^{-1/2}T_s-q^{1/2}\), and direct substitution verifies both bar invariance and \(T_sC_s=-C_s\).',r'The last expression and direct check are elementary consequences clarifying the sign convention; they are not a claimed new result.','E',r'\citet[Sec.~1]{kazhdanlusztig1979representations} uses these two conventions.','Exact; the two bases must not be interchanged in the multiplication formula.',['kazhdanlusztig1979representations'],lc='correct',v='publisher metadata checked')
item(483,'P000906','conjecture','Kazhdan--Lusztig multiplicities of Verma modules',r'(13), twice labelled \ru{гипотеза}, states \([M_w:L_y]=P_{w_0w,w_0y}(1)\), with \(L_w\) the simple quotient and \(y\le w\). The notes say it has been checked for \(\mathrm{GL}(4)\).',r'Let \(\mathfrak g\) be a finite-dimensional complex semisimple Lie algebra, choose a Borel and Cartan, and let \(\lambda\) be a regular integral antidominant weight for the dot action. Put \(M_w=M(w\cdot\lambda)\), and let \(L_w\) be its simple quotient, all in the corresponding block of category \(\mathcal O\). Then \([M_w:L_y]=P_{w_0w,w_0y}(1)\) for \(y\le w\), and the multiplicity is zero otherwise.',r'The regular integral block and antidominant indexing are absent from the notebook and are essential to this exact indexing. The isolated \(\mathrm{GL}(4)\) check is reported as historical notebook evidence, not independently reconstructed.','B',r'Proved independently by \citet{brylinskikashiwara1981holonomic} and \citet{beilinsonbernstein1981localisation}. \citet[pp.~43--44]{brylinski1981differential}, reporting the 1980 Toru\'n lectures, explicitly records both proofs before their 1981 publication.','A theorem in the stated characteristic-zero category. The 1980 note accurately distinguishes the then-conjectural multiplicities from the 1979 basis theorem.',['brylinskikashiwara1981holonomic','beilinsonbernstein1981localisation','brylinski1981differential'],problem='GP-1979-41')
item(483,'P000906','conjecture','Vogan parity and the Ext-polynomial refinement',r'(14)--(15) give \(P_{y,w}=\sum_{i\ge0}q^i\dim\operatorname{Ext}^{\ell(w)-\ell(y)-2i}(M_y,L_w)\) and vanishing in the other parity, attributed to Vogan.',r'With the same regular integral antidominant indexing in category \(\mathcal O\), for \(y\le w\) the dimensions satisfy \[P_{y,w}(q)=\sum_{i\ge0}q^i\dim\operatorname{Ext}^{\ell(w)-\ell(y)-2i}_{\mathcal O}(M_y,L_w),\] taking negative Ext degrees to be zero, and \(\operatorname{Ext}^j_{\mathcal O}(M_y,L_w)=0\) unless \(j\equiv\ell(w)-\ell(y)\pmod2\). Equivalently these are the appropriate weight spaces of nilradical cohomology.',r'The category, block, and indexing convention must be fixed; unrestricted Ext over all \(U(\mathfrak g)\)-modules is not the stated formula.','B',r'\citet[Conj.~3.4 and Thm.~3.5]{vogan1979irreducible} states the equivalent nilradical-cohomology refinement; received 16 July and published December 1979. Its geometric proof belongs to the same 1980--1981 resolution recorded by \citet[pp.~43--44]{brylinski1981differential}.',r'Established under these hypotheses. The coefficient formula gives more information than evaluating at \(q=1\); its equivalence to the character conjecture uses additional structure.',['vogan1979irreducible','brylinski1981differential'],problem='GP-1979-41',pm='exact',conf='high')
item(483,'P000907','theorem','Multiplication in the canonical basis and cell subquotients',r'Equation (17) defines the leading coefficient \(\mu\); \ru{Теорема 1.3}, equation (18), gives the action of \(T_s\). The final sentence says the regular representation becomes block triangular.',r'Let \(\mu(z,v)\) be the coefficient of \(q^{(\ell(v)-\ell(z)-1)/2}\) in \(P_{z,v}\), set to zero when that degree is not a nonnegative integer or \(z\not<v\). Then \[T_sC_v=\begin{cases}-C_v,&sv<v,\\qC_v+q^{1/2}C_{sv}+q^{1/2}\sum_{z<v,\,sz<z}\mu(z,v)C_z,&sv>v.\end{cases}\] The associated preorder yields invariant filtrations and left-cell subquotients of the regular representation. Cell modules are not asserted to be irreducible in every Coxeter type.',r'The zero convention for \(\mu\) makes the notebook sum well-defined. The phrase block triangular is interpreted as a filtration, not a canonical direct-sum decomposition into irreducibles.','E',r'\citet[Thm.~1.3 and the cell construction]{kazhdanlusztig1979representations}; this was already published in June 1979.',r'Established. The formula is for \(C_v\), not \(C^{\prime}_v\).',['kazhdanlusztig1979representations'],v='publisher metadata checked')
Q.append(dict(id='GP-1979-41',title='Kazhdan--Lusztig multiplicity and cohomological conjectures',statement=r'For a regular integral block of category \(\mathcal O\), recover Verma composition multiplicities and the graded Ext dimensions from the Kazhdan--Lusztig polynomials, with the precise antidominant indexing in occurrences 1979-483.6--7.',occurrences=['1979-483.6','1979-483.7'],status=r'As of 10 November 2026: solved. The independent geometric proofs circulated by the 1980 Toru\'n meeting and were published in 1981; see \citet[pp.~43--44]{brylinski1981differential}, \citet{brylinskikashiwara1981holonomic}, and \citet{beilinsonbernstein1981localisation}. This status concerns complex characteristic-zero regular integral category \(\mathcal O\), not unrestricted modular analogues.',status_date='2026-11',refs=['brylinski1981differential','brylinskikashiwara1981holonomic','beilinsonbernstein1981localisation']));save()
corr('P000905','Symmetric-group presentation','The neighboring braid relations generate all the remaining relations.',r'Also include \(s_i s_j=s_j s_i\) for \(|i-j|>1\).','Both source texts omit the distant commuting relations. They do not follow from involutions and the neighboring braid relations for n at least 4.')
corr('P000905','Bruhat subword definition',r'Deleting any subset gives \(w\prime<w\).',r'Use \(w\prime\le w\); require a proper deletion for strict inequality.','The source permits any subset, including the empty subset; this yields w itself.')
corr('P000907','Boundary between records 483 and 484','The catalog page window for Zelevinsky ends at P000906.',r'Theorems (17)--(18) and the cell-representation sentence at the top of P000907 still belong to Zelevinsky; MacPherson starts at his named heading.','The original P000907 scan shows the continuation above the underlined McPherson heading.','provenance')

talk(484,'1980-01-28','R. D. MacPherson','P000906-P000909','Intersection homology and Schubert singularities',r'The actual contribution begins at the MacPherson heading in the lower part of P000907 and ends on that page. It introduces intersection homology, its smooth-space normalization and rational duality, and its interpretation of Kazhdan--Lusztig polynomials. The question mark in the grading shift is preserved as incomplete notation. P000908 begins the 4 February session; it is not a continuation of the January intersection-homology talk. The notebook calls the speaker Bob; his name is Robert D. MacPherson.')
item(484,'P000907','definition','Allowable chains for middle intersection homology',r'(3)--(4) use an even-dimensional filtration and cycles meeting \(X_{n-2c}\) in dimension \(i-c-1\); bounding chains have the bound \(i-c\). The notes mention local topological triviality along strata.',r'Let \(X\) be a pure real \(n\)-dimensional stratified PL pseudomanifold with locally conical structure and no codimension-one stratum. For middle perversity \(\bar m(k)=\lfloor(k-2)/2\rfloor\), the allowable-chain complex consists of PL chains \(\xi\) satisfying \(\dim(|\xi|\cap X_{n-k})\le i-k+\bar m(k)\) and the analogous boundary bound \(\dim(|\partial\xi|\cap X_{n-k})\le i-1-k+\bar m(k)\). Its homology is \(IH_i^{\bar m}(X;\mathbb Q)\). For codimension \(2c\), the first bound is \(i-c-1\).',r'The inequalities, boundary condition, coefficient field and conical pseudomanifold hypotheses are supplied. Equality of dimensions is neither required nor correct.','E',r'Already announced in \citet[definition and propositions]{goreskymacpherson1977dualite}; the full chain treatment appears in \citet[Sec.~1]{goreskymacpherson1980intersection}.','Established. The chain complex, not an unconstrained quotient of all bounding chains, is the precise object.',['goreskymacpherson1977dualite','goreskymacpherson1980intersection'])
item(484,'P000907','theorem','Smooth normalization and rational Poincare duality',r'(1) gives \(IH_i(X)=H_i(X)\) when \(X\) is nonsingular; (2) pairs \(IH_i\) with \(IH_{n-i}\) into \(\mathbb Q\), with an unexplained superscript-like mark.',r'For a manifold the allowable conditions are vacuous, so intersection homology agrees with ordinary homology. For a compact oriented stratified pseudomanifold, complementary perversities \(\bar p(k)+\bar q(k)=k-2\) admit a nondegenerate rational pairing \(IH_i^{\bar p}(X;\mathbb Q)\otimes IH_{n-i}^{\bar q}(X;\mathbb Q)\to\mathbb Q\). If all strata have even codimension, middle perversity is self-complementary and gives the notebook\textquotesingle s same-perversity pairing.',r'Compactness, orientation, rational coefficients and even codimensions are necessary qualifications. No interpretation is forced on the stray superscript. For noncompact spaces use the appropriate compact-support/Borel--Moore pairing.','E',r'\citet[duality theorem]{goreskymacpherson1977dualite}; detailed proof in \citet[Sec.~3.3]{goreskymacpherson1980intersection}.','Established with these hypotheses. Integral perfect duality does not follow in general.',['goreskymacpherson1977dualite','goreskymacpherson1980intersection'])
item(484,'P000907','construction','Schubert closures supply the relevant even stratification',r'The note first says strange cohomology of Schubert cells, then explicitly writes \ru{шуберт. кл. (замкн.)} and uses smaller cells as the strata.',r'For a connected complex reductive group \(G\), Borel \(B\), Weyl group \(W\), and \(t\in W\), set \(X_t=\overline{BtB/B}\subset G/B\). Its Bruhat cells \(BsB/B\), \(s\le t\), are smooth affine complex spaces of dimensions \(\ell(s)\); they give the even real-dimensional stratification used for the local intersection-cohomology construction.',r'The singular space is a Schubert variety, the closure of a cell. An open Bruhat cell itself is smooth and cannot carry the claimed singularity information.','E',r'This is the geometry underlying \citet{kazhdanlusztig1980schubert}; the published volume records a March 1979 conference, but the exact first circulation date of this formulation is not established here.','The closure/open-cell distinction is essential.',['kazhdanlusztig1980schubert'],v='publisher metadata checked')
item(484,'P000907','theorem','Local intersection cohomology computes Kazhdan--Lusztig polynomials',r'(5) writes \(P_{s,t}(q)=q^{?}\sum_i\dim IH_i(X_t,X_t-y_s)q^i\). The exponent is literally a question mark; the use of special characteristic-\(p\) cohomology is mentioned.',r'For \(s\le t\) in the Weyl group of a complex reductive group, let \(IC_{X_t}\) be the rational intersection-cohomology complex normalized to restrict to \(\mathbb Q[\ell(t)]\) on the open cell. At \(y_s\) in the \(s\)-cell, \[P_{s,t}(q)=\sum_{j\ge0}\dim\mathcal H^{2j-\ell(t)}(IC_{X_t})_{y_s}\,q^j.\] Stalk degrees of the opposite parity vanish. This is a local stalk formula, not the ordinary or global Poincare polynomial of \(X_t\).',r'The displayed formula is an editorial modern normalization, replacing the visibly unfinished homological notation. No value is silently inserted for the original question mark; homological local groups require duality and indexing translations.','B',r'The exact geometric identification is \citet{kazhdanlusztig1980schubert}, published 1980 in the proceedings of the March 1979 meeting. Bibliographic identity is checked; the original full proof was not accessible in this research pass.','An established characteristic-zero geometric interpretation. The notebook records the theorem with an unresolved grading convention.',['kazhdanlusztig1980schubert'],lc='not determinable',v='publisher metadata checked',conf='high')
corr('P000907','Intersection-chain dimension conditions',r'The intersection has dimension exactly \(i-c-1\), or \(i-c\) for a bounding chain.',r'Read \(\le i-c-1\) and \(\le i-c\), and require the boundary to be allowable.','The scan contains no inequality sign. The allowable-chain definition in Goresky--MacPherson 1977/1980 uses upper bounds; disjoint intersections are allowed.')
corr('P000907','Local polynomial formula',r'\(q^{?}\sum_i\dim IH_i(X_t,X_t-y_s)q^i\).',r'Leave the notebook exponent unresolved; use an explicitly labelled normalized intersection-cohomology stalk formula in the companion.','The question mark is visible in the original scan. Its repair requires a grading convention, not paleographic substitution.','ambiguity')
corr('P000907-P000908','MacPherson date boundary','Record 484 nominal window P000906-P000909.',r'The 28 January contribution occupies the lower P000907; P000908 explicitly starts 4 February with Olshanskii, followed by a separate MacPherson talk.','Date and speaker headings in the scans determine the occurrence attribution.','provenance')

talk(485,'1980-02-04','G. I. Olshanskii','P000908','Unitary representations of infinite classical groups',r'The upper part of P000908 describes a multiparameter family for the infinite real general linear group, with finite-multiplicity discrete restriction to its orthogonal subgroup, and type-I/classification results for completed groups of fixed finite real rank. The completion topology and the distinction between the 1978 type-I theorem and the more complete 1980 construction are indispensable. MacPherson begins a separate contribution below the named heading on the same page.')
item(485,'P000908','construction','The Gaussian family with orthogonal-group labels',r'(1)--(3) display \(G=\bigcup GL(n,\mathbb R)\), \(K=\bigcup SO(n)\), and \(T_{\pi_1,s_1;\ldots;\pi_r,s_r}\), where \(\pi_i\in\widehat{O(n_i)}\) and \(s_i\in\mathbb R\).',r'For the Gaussian quasi-regular representation \(T_s\) of the finite-matrix group \(SL(\infty,\mathbb R)\) on \(L^2(\mathbb R^{\mathbb N},\mu)\), the commutant of \(T_s^{\otimes n}\) is generated by the natural \(O(n)\) action mixing the rows. Its \(O(n)\)-isotypic multiplicity spaces carry irreducible representations \(T_{\pi,s}\). For pairwise distinct real parameters \(s_i\), \(\bigotimes_iT_{\pi_i,s_i}\) is irreducible and determines the unordered parameter family. These representations extend to the polar completion \(GL(\infty,\mathbb R)=UR\), where \(U\) has the strong operator topology and \(R\) consists of positive invertible operators \(1+A\) with \(A\) Hilbert--Schmidt, with its Hilbert--Schmidt topology.',r'The Gaussian model, extension topology and distinctness condition are reconstructed from the identified publication. The notebook gives only the parameter list, not a classification of all abstract representations of the algebraic union.','A',r'Exact family in \citet[Secs.~3 and 5, Thms.~5--7]{olshanskii1980construction}; presented 21 June and received 25 June 1979, published in volume 250(2), 1980. The exact issue-release day relative to 4 February was not verified.','A proved contemporary construction. The A label identifies the speaker\textquotesingle s 1980 publication; it does not imply that the manuscript first circulated after the seminar.',['olshanskii1980construction'])
item(485,'P000908','claim','Discrete finite-multiplicity restriction and the maximal-compact analogy',r'The family restricts to \(K\) with \ru{конечнокр. дискр. спектр}; the notes call \(K\) analogous to a maximal compact subgroup and mention a unitary variant.',r'For these Gaussian finite-row representations, orthogonal transformations of the countably many columns preserve the Gaussian measure, so the restriction is independent of the parameters \(s_i\). Its Hilbert decomposition into continuous tensor-type \(SO(\infty)\) representations is discrete, with finite multiplicities. The complex construction replaces real Gaussian space and \(O(n_i)\) labels by their complex/unitary counterparts. The union \(SO(\infty)\) is not itself a compact group.',r'The assertion is restricted to the family displayed in the notes and to its continuous tensor category; it is not a Peter--Weyl theorem for all representations of an arbitrary inductive-limit topology.','A',r'\citet[Sec.~3 and the proof of Thm.~6]{olshanskii1980construction} supplies the Gaussian/compact-dual model; compare the continuous compact-limit decomposition in \citet[Sec.~1]{olshanskii1978unitary}.','Established in this representation class. The maximal-compact phrase is an analogy about restriction theory.',['olshanskii1980construction','olshanskii1978unitary'],pm='partial',conf='medium')
item(485,'P000908','theorem','Type I for the completed finite-real-rank groups',r'(4)--(6) put closure bars over the unions \(SO(p,n)\), \(U(p,n)\), \(Sp(p,n)\), call them completions, and state that they are type I.',r'Fix finite \(p\). Use Olshanskii\textquotesingle s operator completions of \(SO_0(p,\infty)\), \(U(p,\infty)\), and \(Sp(p,\infty)\), not the discrete group or an unspecified final topology. Every continuous unitary representation generates a type-I von Neumann algebra; there are no factor representations of type II or III. In the norm/strong formulations specified by the paper, the continuous representation categories agree.',r'The identity component \(SO_0\), Hilbert-space continuity and completion topology are supplied. Type I refers to the generated operator algebra of every continuous unitary representation.','E',r'\citet[Thm.~3.3]{olshanskii1978unitary}; received 20 October 1977, published 1978. The 1980 paper explicitly credits this earlier result.','Established for the stated completions. Changing the topology changes the assertion.',['olshanskii1978unitary'])
item(485,'P000908','theorem','Exhaustion of finite-rank irreducibles by holomorphic constructions',r'The same sentence says that all irreducible representations of the completed fixed-\(p\) groups have been described.',r'For the completed groups in the preceding item, the 1980 classification uses irreducible holomorphic representations of a specified \(\mathbb Z\)-cover of \(U(p,\infty)\). Their restrictions produce the irreducibles of \(SO_0(p,\infty)\) and, from \(U(2p,\infty)\), of \(Sp(p,\infty)\); holomorphic--antiholomorphic tensor products give the unitary case. The admissible highest-weight pairs in Theorems 1--2 determine the parameters. For the un-covered \(U(p,\infty)\), retain precisely the representations on which the covering kernel acts trivially.',r'The cover, admissibility and descent condition are absent from the one-line notebook summary. We state the classification mechanism, not an unsupported claim for arbitrary representations of the algebraic union.','A',r'\citet[Thms.~1--4]{olshanskii1980construction} proves the exhaustion; manuscript June 1979, publication 1980. The 1978 paper proves type I and constructs many representations but does not claim this full exhaustion.','Established in the stated continuous category; the two chronology layers are kept separate.',['olshanskii1980construction','olshanskii1978unitary'])

talk(486,'1980-02-04','R. D. MacPherson','P000909-P000914','Combinatorial Pontryagin classes and the dilogarithm',r'The contribution begins below Olshanskii on P000908 and occupies P000908--P000913. Most of the construction is identifiable in MacPherson\textquotesingle s February 1977 Bourbaki exposition, published in 1978: locally linear stars, hypersimplices, configuration complexes, the compatible product complex and the signed \(1/48\) cocycle for \(p_1\). The end moves to Grassmannians and a dilogarithmic primitive, developed with Gelfand in their 1982 paper. The 11 February heading on P000914 begins the next talk. This is a return to the characteristic-class line in occurrences 1976-413.5--11, not a continuation of the January intersection-homology discussion.')
item(486,'P000908','definition','Locally linear flattenings are an additional triangulation hypothesis',r'\(X\) is a simplicial four-manifold, and for every simplex \(\sigma\) the notes assume \(\psi:\operatorname{St}\sigma\to\mathbb R^4\), linear on simplices and homeomorphic near the interior of \(\sigma\). A two-dimensional sketch illustrates the star.',r'Work with a finite triangulated PL four-manifold, with a local flattening of each simplex: a simplicewise affine map from its star to \(\mathbb R^4\) that is a homeomorphism on a neighborhood of the relative interior. Equivalently, the normal cone on the link of a codimension-\(k\) simplex has an appropriate linear flattening in \(\mathbb R^k\). This condition is imposed on the triangulation; it is not inferred merely from the word PL.',r'The normal-space formulation explains the later dependence on codimension. A chosen triangulation of a PL manifold need not automatically satisfy this particular linear-star condition.','E',r'\citet[pp.~105--106]{macpherson1978combinatorial}; exposition February 1977, publication 1978.','A precise hypothesis of the construction, not a new 1980 assertion of universal linear realizability.',['macpherson1978combinatorial'])
item(486,'P000909','construction','The hypersimplex over a simplex and its elementary models',r'(5)--(6) prescribe \(h(v)\in[0,1]\), \(h=1\) on vertices of \(\sigma\), and \(\sum_vh(v)=5\). Examples are a point, a segment, a triangle and an octahedron. Vertices are labelled by the places where \(h=1\).',r'For a simplex \(\sigma\) in a closed four-manifold, let \(V_\sigma\) be the vertex set of its star and put \[H_\sigma=\{h\in[0,1]^{V_\sigma}:h|_{V(\sigma)}=1,\ \sum h=5\}.\] If \(\sigma\) has codimension \(k\), the remaining coordinates sum to \(k\). All vertices are zero--one functions. Thus a top-dimensional simplex gives a point; a codimension-one simplex gives a segment. For codimension two, three remaining vertices give \(\Delta(2,3)\), a triangle, and four give \(\Delta(2,4)\), an octahedron.',r'The notebook uses two-dimensional pictures for four-dimensional normal combinatorics. The exact cube slice is \(\sum h_i=2\), not an arbitrary equidistant cutting plane.','E',r'\citet[Sec.~1]{macpherson1978combinatorial}; compare the earlier seminar occurrence 1976-413.10.','An elementary convex-polytope construction. Boundary simplices require a relative variant of the segment example.',['macpherson1978combinatorial'])
item(486,'P000908-P000909','construction','Gluing the hypersimplicial space over the original complex',r'For \(\sigma\subset\sigma^{\prime}\), the notes put \(H_{\sigma^{\prime}}\subset H_\sigma\), identify the common part \(H_{\sigma^{\prime}}\times\sigma\), and glue the products. They also draw the original and dual cell structures.',r'Extend a function on \(V_{\sigma^{\prime}}\) by zero to \(V_\sigma\); this embeds \(H_{\sigma^{\prime}}\) as a face of \(H_\sigma\). Glue the products \(H_\sigma\times\sigma\) along these face inclusions to obtain \(H\to X\). Use the barycentric/common subdivision \(X^{\prime}\) when comparing the primal simplex cells with the dual cells \(\sigma^D\); cellular means that each skeleton maps into the corresponding skeleton.',r'The extension-by-zero rule and the common subdivision make the arrows in the notebook unambiguous.','E',r'\citet[Sec.~1]{macpherson1978combinatorial}.','Established; incidence reverses under passage from simplices to dual cells.',['macpherson1978combinatorial'])
item(486,'P000910','definition','Configuration spaces and their degeneracy loci',r'\(C_\sigma\) first means all flattenings \(\psi\). General position requires every set consisting of all vertices of \(\sigma\) and \(k\) other star vertices, five points in total, to be independent. Speciality \(c\) counts the failures.',r'For a codimension-\(k\) simplex, pass to normal flattenings of the cone on its link in \(\mathbb R^k\), modulo \(GL(k,\mathbb R)\). A configuration is generic if each relevant \(k\)-subset of link vertices has nonzero determinant. The \(c\)-fold degeneracy locus has exactly \(c\) zero determinants. It is semialgebraic and has finitely many connected components. In the original four-space, these are the affine-independence tests on the displayed five points.',r'The normal quotient and the word exactly are supplied. The notes subsequently reuse \(C_\sigma\) for a finite cell model; the raw realization space and that model are distinct.','E',r'\citet[Sec.~2]{macpherson1978combinatorial}.','A precise finite semialgebraic construction. No assertion that all its connected pieces are balls follows from the definition.',['macpherson1978combinatorial'])
item(486,'P000910','question','Does the degeneracy decomposition itself form a cell structure?',r'After defining speciality the recorder writes \ru{Неизвестно: стратификация или клет. разбиение}.',r'The identifiable issue is whether the degeneracy pieces of the raw flattening quotient, or their connected components, have the topology and incidence properties of cells. The notes do not specify which decomposition, whether a refinement is allowed, or what notion of stratification is intended. Therefore they do not determine a unique yes/no theorem.',r'This uncertainty is retained rather than converted into a blanket conjecture about all realization spaces. Semialgebraic refinement and the separately constructed finite complex answer different questions.','F',r'\citet[Sec.~2]{macpherson1978combinatorial} explicitly constructs a cell complex from components; it does not identify every raw component with an open cell.','No exact standalone problem can be recovered from this sentence. It is not evidence that the whole Pontryagin construction remained undeveloped.',['macpherson1978combinatorial'],lc='not determinable',pm='partial',conf='medium')
item(486,'P000910-P000911','construction','The low-codimension configuration cell models',r'For \(k=4\), one point is assigned to every generic component; for \(k=3\), walls of speciality one produce edges; for \(k=2\), speciality-two components give squares. The later point case mistakenly repeats \(k=2\).',r'Write \(K_\sigma\) for the finite model. For codimension four it is a discrete set. For codimension three it is the graph with vertices the generic components and an edge for each connected one-degeneracy wall, joining the two adjacent components. For codimension two fill the four-cycle around a two-degeneracy component by a square. For codimension one or zero put \(K_\sigma=\{*\}\). Crossing one determinant wall changes a sign, hence its two endpoints are distinct and no loop edge arises.',r'The notation \(K_\sigma\) separates the model from the raw configuration space. The second point-case index must be zero, not two.','E',r'\citet[Sec.~2, pp.~109--111]{macpherson1978combinatorial}.','The stated finite construction is established. It is not a theorem that arbitrary higher-codimension realization spaces have such a simple cell decomposition.',['macpherson1978combinatorial'],lc='false as written')
item(486,'P000911','theorem','Connectivity of the codimension-three configuration graph',r'The first line says \ru{Граф связен (трудная теорема!)}.',r'For a codimension-three simplex in the locally flattenable triangulated manifold under consideration, the configuration graph described in the preceding item is connected: generic local flattenings can be joined by passages through one-degeneracy walls.',r'The assertion is about this local-star graph. It is not a general connectivity claim for all oriented-matroid realization spaces.','E',r'\citet[Note~2, p.~122]{macpherson1978combinatorial} explicitly supplies the connectivity justification via Cairns\textquotesingle s 1944 isotopy theorem.','Established in the indicated setting; the difficult step was already available before the talk.',['macpherson1978combinatorial'],pm='exact')
item(486,'P000911','construction','Restriction maps glue the configuration models',r'The margin asks whether the \(C_\sigma\times\sigma^D\) glue naturally; the next lines restrict \(\psi\) from a larger star to a smaller one and draw \(C_{\sigma^{\prime}}\to C_\sigma\) when \(\sigma^{\prime}\subset\sigma\).',r'If \(\sigma^{\prime}\subset\sigma\), restriction followed by projection to the corresponding normal quotient induces a map of configuration spaces and of the finite cell models \(K_{\sigma^{\prime}}\to K_\sigma\). These compatible maps glue the products with dual cells to a cellular space \(C\to X^D\). Using \(X^{\prime}\), it can be combined with \(H\to X\).',r'The normal projection, needed because codimension changes, is implicit in the notebook word restriction. The question is answered immediately by the construction rather than left as a newly open problem.','E',r'\citet[Sec.~2]{macpherson1978combinatorial}.','Established. This is related to the compatibility theme of 1976-413.11 but does not restate a new unsolved boundary-chain problem.',['macpherson1978combinatorial'])
item(486,'P000908-P000911','construction','The compatible product complex Gamma',r'The notes draw \(\Gamma\subset H\times_XC\). The bottom of P000911 says a product is included when the five-point configurations specified by vertices of the hypersimplex face are generic.',r'Over the common subdivision, retain a product cell of the hypersimplicial and configuration factors only when every basis-sized subset indicated by a vertex of its hypersimplex face is nondegenerate for the corresponding flattening component. The resulting compatible cell subcomplex \(\Gamma\) has a projection \(\pi:\Gamma\to X\). Its allowed incidences make the characteristic cocycle well-defined.',r'The handwritten membership description is compressed; the criterion is supplied in the vocabulary of the published construction. It does not imply that every product cell is admissible.','E',r'\citet[Sec.~3]{macpherson1978combinatorial}.','An established compatibility construction; the projection is not claimed to be an ordinary fiber bundle.',['macpherson1978combinatorial'])
item(486,'P000912','construction','The signed one-forty-eighth cocycle on four-cells',r'The four face types have five variable coordinates and respectively three, two, one or zero additional coordinates fixed at one, above a vertex of \(X\). The two extreme types have value zero; the middle types have \(\widetilde P=\pm1/48\), with sign \((-1)^i\) from ten orientations.',r'For an oriented four-cell lying over a vertex, a four-dimensional hypersimplex face has five varying coordinates and \(b=0,1,2,3\) other coordinates fixed at one besides the base vertex. The varying coordinates sum to \(4-b\), giving \(\Delta(4,5),\Delta(3,5),\Delta(2,5),\Delta(1,5)\). The characteristic cochain vanishes on the extreme types and on the other prescribed four-cells. On the middle types project the five varying vertices along the fixed span to \(\mathbb R^2\) or \(\mathbb R^3\). With the cell orientation determined by their ordering, its value is \((-1)^i/48\), where \(i\) counts positively oriented ordered pairs or triples among the ten relevant minors.',r'The base must be a vertex for this displayed fixed-coordinate count. Reversing the auxiliary orientation replaces \(i\) by \(10-i\), so the sign is unchanged; reversing the cell orientation reverses the value.','E',r'\citet[Sec.~4]{macpherson1978combinatorial}.','An explicit rational cochain in the specified orientation convention. The normalization is not a universal assertion about all Pontryagin cocycle models.',['macpherson1978combinatorial'])
item(486,'P000909-P000912','theorem','The combinatorial cocycle represents the first Pontryagin class',r'(4) says \(\pi^*p^1(X)=[\widetilde P]\), and the intervening construction is meant to produce \(\widetilde P\in C^4(\Gamma)\).',r'Under the local flattening hypotheses, the prescribed cochain is a cocycle and \([\widetilde P]=\pi^*p_1(TX)\in H^4(\Gamma;\mathbb Q)\). A homological four-section \(s\) of \(\pi\) yields a representative \(s^*\widetilde P\) of \(p_1(X)\). Such a section is a chain map right-inverse through degree four; it is not necessarily a continuous section of \(\pi\).',r'The rational coefficients and homological-section interpretation explain how the pulled-back identity computes a class on \(X\). These details come from the publication, not an additional theorem claimed in the notebook.','E',r'\citet[Sec.~3, Propositions~1--2; Sec.~4, theorem and Corollary~1]{macpherson1978combinatorial}.',r'Proved before 1980. The later all-Pontryagin formula of \citet{gelfandmacpherson1992combinatorial} is a broader development, not the publication date of this first-class formula.',['macpherson1978combinatorial','gelfandmacpherson1992combinatorial'])
item(486,'P000912','claim','Six coordinate degeneracy divisors on the real Grassmannian',r'The notes mention six Schubert-cell closures, six coordinate two-planes in \(\mathbb R^4\), and planes nontransverse to each of them.',r'For each coordinate two-plane \(E_I\subset\mathbb R^4\), the locus \(D_I=\{L\in\mathrm{Gr}(2,4):L\cap E_I\ne0\}\) is a Schubert degeneracy hypersurface for a flag containing \(E_I\). There are six such coordinate choices; their complement is the locus where all six Plucker coordinates are nonzero. Separately, the Schubert decomposition for one fixed full flag has six cells of dimensions \(0,1,2,2,3,4\). The six coordinate divisors are not those six cell closures.',r'The source compresses two uses of six. The distinction is supplied without silently equating the coordinate hypersurfaces with all cells for one flag.','E',r'The Grassmannian/configuration setting is in \citet[Sec.~6]{macpherson1978combinatorial}; the precise generic-minor locus is also described in \citet[Secs.~3.1--3.3]{rojas2026explicit}.','Standard incidence geometry. The notebook wording does not determine which full flags were intended.',['macpherson1978combinatorial','rojas2026explicit'],pm='partial',conf='medium')
item(486,'P000912-P000913','construction','Enhanced configurations of four rays and cross-ratio',r'The English phrase says four points in \(\mathbb RP^2\), but the next lines and figure say four rays in \(\mathbb R^2\), ordinary points in \(\mathbb RP^1\), and \(\mathbb R^4/L\) for a two-plane \(L\). Configurations are equivalent under \(GL(2,\mathbb R)\).',r'For \(L\in\mathrm{Gr}(2,4)\) with all relevant minors nonzero, the images of the four positive coordinate rays in \(\mathbb R^4/L\) are nonzero rays in a two-dimensional space. Up to linear isomorphism, they form an enhanced projective configuration: a projective-line configuration together with choices of lifts from lines to rays. For four distinct labelled projective points, an ordered cross-ratio is a complete continuous projective invariant; the ray choices add discrete data.',r'Projectivization has dimension one. The genericity condition excludes zero quotient vectors and coincident projective points. Cross-ratio alone does not encode all enhancement signs.','E',r'\citet[Secs.~6--7]{macpherson1978combinatorial}.','Established. The configuration quotient may have non-Hausdorff boundary behavior; the generic interval components are sufficient for the cross-ratio discussion.',['macpherson1978combinatorial'],lc='false as written')
item(486,'P000913','construction','A Pontryagin form on the Grassmannian',r'The notes label \(P\) a four-form representing the Pontryagin class of the standard rank-two bundle \(\xi\) on \(G_{2,2}(\mathbb R)\), and say that a connection is constructed.',r'Let \(\xi\) be the tautological real two-plane bundle on \(\mathrm{Gr}(2,4)\), with the metric and projected connection induced from Euclidean space. Its Chern--Weil representative is \(P=-\frac{1}{8\pi^2}\operatorname{tr}(\Omega\wedge\Omega)\), where \(\Omega\) is the curvature. The de Rham class is \(p_1(\xi)\), independent of the connection. On the oriented double cover it equals the square of the Euler class.',r'The connection, curvature normalization and orientation-cover observation supply a precise version of the note. If instead one uses the quotient bundle under the complementary-plane identification, the corresponding sign/convention must be tracked.','E',r'\citet[Secs.~5 and 8]{macpherson1978combinatorial} uses the invariant Grassmannian characteristic form.','Classical Chern--Weil theory; its later fiber integral is a different operation.',['macpherson1978combinatorial'])
item(486,'P000913','claim','Fiber integration of the Pontryagin form and the Rogers dilogarithm',r'The scan, more clearly than the linearized transcription, gives \(\bar\pi_*(P)=d\Phi\) and labels \(\Phi\) a function, \ru{дилогарифм}. The projection sends a two-plane to its enhanced four-ray configuration.',r'On a generic component of the configuration quotient of \(\mathrm{Gr}(2,4)\), integration of the invariant first-Pontryagin four-form along the three-dimensional diagonal-scaling fibers, with the hypersimplicial compactification and an orientation convention, gives a one-form proportional to \(dL_R(x)\), where \(x\) is cross-ratio and \[L_R(x)=\operatorname{Re}\operatorname{Li}_2(x)+\tfrac12\log|x|\log|1-x|,\qquad x\in\mathbb R\setminus\{0,1\}.\] Thus locally the fiber integral has a dilogarithmic primitive. Its scalar and additive constants depend on fiber/sign and normalization choices.',r'The source supplies neither the compactification nor a scalar normalization. The statement uses the real Rogers dilogarithm, not the Bloch--Wigner function; it does not silently assert an equality for an unspecified arbitrary connection.','A',r'Developed by \citet[Secs.~0 and 3]{gelfandmacpherson1982geometry}, published June 1982. The full modern computation was read in \citet[Sec.~3.7 and Thm.~3.7, case \(k=1\)]{rojas2026explicit}, preprint 26 October 2026. The 1982 paper\textquotesingle s publisher metadata, not its full proof, was checked.',r'The first-class relation is established. The 2026 preprint supplies a new explicit treatment of powers of \(p_1\); its broader claims are not substituted for the notebook\textquotesingle s elementary case.',['gelfandmacpherson1982geometry','rojas2026explicit'],pm='exact',lc='correct with missing hypotheses',conf='high')
corr('P000911','Point cases of the configuration complex',r'\(k=1,k=2\Rightarrow C_\sigma\) is a point.',r'\(k=1,k=0\Rightarrow K_\sigma\) is a point.','The original scan clearly repeats k=2, immediately after defining a two-dimensional complex for that case. MacPherson 1978, Sec.2, says codimension 1 or 0. The transcription is faithful; the mathematics is emended.')
corr('P000912','Enhanced projective configuration dimension',r'Four points in \(\mathbb RP^2\).',r'Four rays in \(\mathbb R^2\), or enhanced points of \(\mathbb RP^1\).','The exponent 2 is visible in the scan; its own next line and P000913 give the quotient R4/L with dim L=2 and explicitly draw four rays in R2.')
corr('P000908-P000914','MacPherson February contribution boundaries','Record 486 nominally P000909-P000914.',r'The 4 February contribution starts at the MacPherson heading on P000908, ends on P000913, and is followed by a separately dated 11 February contribution on P000914.','Original dated headings and speaker change on P000908/P000914.','provenance')

talk(487,'1980-02-11','R. D. MacPherson','P000915','Grassmannians, hypersimplices and enhanced configurations',r'The actual source is the short, separately dated fragment P000914; P000915 starts Zakharov on 18 February. It states the idea that a Grassmannian point is determined by a hypersimplicial datum and an enhanced projective configuration, with \(n+k\) labelled vectors in a \(k\)-dimensional space. The projected-space dimension is written incorrectly, and the bottom of the fragment is clipped. The finite statement is already present in the 1977/1978 Bourbaki exposition; no missing continuation is invented.')
item(487,'P000914','construction','From a Grassmannian point to a vector and ray configuration',r'\(G_{n,k}\) consists of unoriented \(n\)-planes in \(\mathbb R^{n+k}\); the text defines enhanced configurations of \(n+k\) points in \(k\)-space, parenthetically calling its projective dimension \(k+1\).',r'For \(L\in\mathrm{Gr}(n,n+k)\), take the images of the coordinate vectors in \(V=\mathbb R^{n+k}/L\), of dimension \(k\), modulo linear isomorphisms of \(V\). The resulting spanning labelled vector configuration determines \(L\) as the kernel of the corresponding surjection \(\mathbb R^{n+k}\to V\). Passing to the images of positive coordinate rays forgets their positive lengths and gives an enhanced projective configuration; zero quotient vectors need a separate boundary stratum. Nonzero projective points lie in \(\mathbb RP^{k-1}\).',r'The kernel construction, generic/nonzero qualification, and dimension \(k-1\) are editorial clarification. The printed \(k+1\) is not retained as a mathematical claim.','E',r'\citet[Sec.~6]{macpherson1978combinatorial}, exposition February 1977 and publication 1978.','An exact quotient/configuration construction with the stated boundary qualification.',['macpherson1978combinatorial'],lc='false as written')
item(487,'P000914','claim','A configuration plus a hypersimplex point recovers the plane',r'The fragment\textquotesingle s central line says \ru{Элементу} \(G_{n,k}\) \ru{соотв. пара гиперсимплекс + конфигурация}.',r'Fix a spanning rank-\(k\) vector configuration \(w_1,\ldots,w_{n+k}\) and denote by \(\Delta_I\) its maximal minors. A convenient hypersimplicial coordinate is \[h_i=\frac{\sum_{I\ni i}|\Delta_I|}{\sum_{|I|=k}|\Delta_I|},\qquad0\le h_i\le1,\quad\sum_i h_i=k.\] On each orbit of positive independent coordinate rescalings modulo common scale, this map is a homeomorphism onto the relative interior of the polytope spanned by the indicator vectors of subsets with \(\Delta_I\ne0\); its closure maps to that polytope. Thus the enhanced projective configuration and this point recover the Grassmannian datum. For a generic configuration the polytope is the full \(\Delta(k,n+k)\).',r'The explicit map, absolute-minor convention and non-generic face polytope come from the identified source, not the clipped continuation. Alternative moment-map normalizations using squared minors give related coordinates and are not conflated with this formula.','E',r'\citet[Sec.~6, proposition]{macpherson1978combinatorial}; the further Grassmannian development is \citet{gelfandmacpherson1982geometry}.','Established in the specified configuration/orbit formulation. The notebook does not formulate a new open question here.',['macpherson1978combinatorial','gelfandmacpherson1982geometry'])
corr('P000914','Projectivization dimension',r'\(k\)-dimensional vector space or \((k+1)\)-dimensional projective space.',r'A \(k\)-dimensional vector space projectivizes to dimension \(k-1\).','The scan clearly reads k+1, and the accompanying count uses a k-dimensional quotient. This is a faithful notebook dimensional error.')
corr('P000914-P000915','Record 487 page window','Catalog record 487 points to P000915.',r'The 11 February MacPherson fragment is P000914; P000915 explicitly names Zakharov and 18 February 1980.','The original page headings establish both dates and speakers.','provenance')

talk(488,'1980-02-18','V. E. Zakharov','P000916-P000918','Benney equations, conservation laws and Hamiltonian variables',r'The actual session occupies P000915--P000916. Starting from an incompressible fluid with a free surface, Zakharov takes the hydrostatic long-wave approximation and announces additional conservation laws, simpler Hamiltonian variables, and an extension to two horizontal dimensions. Gelfand interprets the model as collective dynamics through a common field. The contemporary papers supply precise versions of the short announcements; no theorem of complete integrability is attributed to these notes. P000917 explicitly starts 22 September 1980, with Fuchs on intersection homology; P000918 continues that discussion and turns to Graev on integral geometry. Those next-year items are inventoried separately in \texttt{code/boundary\_inventory.md} and are not occurrences of this record.')
item(488,'P000915-P000916','method','Hydrostatic reduction of the incompressible free-surface equations',r'The fluid occupies \(0<z<h(r,t)\), with horizontal velocity \(u\), vertical velocity \(w\), and \(w_z+\operatorname{div}u=0\). The vertical equation on P000915 has \(-p_z+1\), while P000916 writes \(p_z+1=0\) and obtains \(p=h-z\).',r'For an inviscid incompressible fluid of constant density, a flat impermeable bed, gravitational acceleration scaled to one, and zero pressure at the surface, neglecting the entire vertical material acceleration gives \(p_z=-1\), hence \(p(r,z,t)=h(r,t)-z\). The horizontal equation is \[u_t+(u\cdot\nabla_r)u+w u_z+\nabla_r h=0,\qquad w_z+\operatorname{div}_r u=0.\] This is the hydrostatic long-wave model, not the full free-surface Euler system.',r'The pressure sign, surface-pressure condition and scaling assumptions are made explicit. The bracket marking terms to neglect in the notebook does not include every vertical-acceleration term; \(w_t\) must also be negligible for the displayed reduction.','E',r'The long-wave system is the starting point, credited to Benney, in \citet[Sec.~1, Eqs.~(1)--(4)]{zakharov1980benney} and \citet[Sec.~1]{zakharov1981benney}. Their introduction identifies the previously studied model, while their new claims concern its structure.','The formal reduction is established under the stated approximation. Its validity over a specified error scale and time interval requires a separate asymptotic justification.',['zakharov1980benney','zakharov1981benney'],lc='false as written')
item(488,'P000916','theorem','The height equation is integrated mass conservation',r'The third displayed equation is \(h_t+\operatorname{div}\int_0^h u\,dz=0\); the notes say that the one-dimensional system had been studied by Manin and others.',r'If \(h>0\) and \(u,w,h\) are smooth, impose \(w(r,0,t)=0\) and the kinematic free-surface condition \(h_t+u(r,h,t)\cdot\nabla_r h=w(r,h,t)\). Integrating incompressibility and differentiating the moving upper limit yields \[h_t+\operatorname{div}_r\int_0^{h(r,t)}u(r,z,t)\,dz=0.\] Together with the horizontal equation and incompressibility, this is the displayed Benney system.',r'The bed and free-surface boundary conditions are required to derive the equation; they are not all written in the fragment.','E',r'\citet[Sec.~1]{zakharov1980benney} and \citet[Eqs.~(1)--(4)]{zakharov1981benney} explicitly start from this pre-existing long-wave system.','An exact mass balance within the smooth hydrostatic model. It does not require any assertion of complete integrability.',['zakharov1980benney','zakharov1981benney'])
item(488,'P000916','claim','Additional branches generate conservation laws beyond the usual moment hierarchy',r'Zakharov announces \ru{счетное число законов сохранения (гораздо больше, чем было)}: a countable collection, substantially more than previously known. No densities are written.',r'A precise finite-layer reconstruction is \(\rho_{a,t}+(\rho_a u_a)_x=0\), \(u_{a,t}+u_a u_{a,x}+h_x=0\), \(h=\sum_{a=1}^{N}\rho_a\), with smooth fields and distinct velocities on the region considered. Set \[R(p)=p+\sum_{a=1}^{N}\frac{\rho_a}{p-u_a}.\] Each formal inverse branch \(R(p)=\lambda\) satisfies \(p_t+\partial_x(p^2/2+h)=0\). Expansion of the branch at infinity yields the standard moment densities; the additional branches approaching the \(u_a\) give further conservation-law sequences. Their integrals are conserved for periodic fields or suitable decay.',r'The finite-layer system and formal expansions are reconstructed from the identified papers. The continuum-label limit needs additional analytic or formal-series hypotheses and, for the displayed non-polynomial formulas in the paper, a specified principal-value prescription. The notebook does not state a completeness theorem for these integrals.','A',r'\citet[Sec.~4, Eqs.~(48)--(52)]{zakharov1980benney}, received 17 December 1979 and published in 1980, develops the announcement; \citet[Sec.~4, Eqs.~(35)--(39)]{zakharov1981benney} displays the extra branches and involution argument. The latter explicitly says that completeness, needed for full integrability, is not proved.','The formal conservation hierarchies are established for the stated reductions. They do not by themselves prove global solvability or complete integrability of every smooth Benney solution.',['zakharov1980benney','zakharov1981benney'],pm='partial')
item(488,'P000916','construction','Lagrangian layers give a simpler one-dimensional Hamiltonian bracket',r'Zakharov announces \ru{более простая гамильтонова структура}, and says that the idea is a reinterpretation of the equation.',r'Use a material vertical label \(a\) and a smooth monotone height coordinate \(z=z(x,a,t)\) with \(\rho=z_a>0\). In a fixed label interval, let \(h=\int\rho\,da\). The equations become \(\rho_t+\partial_x(\rho u)=0\), \(u_t+u u_x+h_x=0\). With periodic or decaying fields, \[E=\tfrac12\int\!\left(\int\rho u^2\,da+h^2\right)dx,\qquad \binom{\rho_t}{u_t}=-\begin{pmatrix}0&\partial_x\\\partial_x&0\end{pmatrix}\binom{\delta E/\delta\rho}{\delta E/\delta u},\] where the bracket includes \(\delta(a-b)\) in the label variable. Thus the bracket has constant coefficients in these variables.',r'The precise fields, energy, boundary conditions and bracket are absent from the notes. The coordinate change is valid only while the layers remain smooth and the vertical parametrization stays invertible.','A',r'\citet[Sec.~2]{zakharov1980benney}, manuscript received December 1979, and \citet[p.~195, Eqs.~(11)--(12)]{zakharov1981benney} give this Hamiltonian formulation.','Established locally in these smooth variables. This simple bracket must not be confused with a proof of existence of global smooth solutions.',['zakharov1980benney','zakharov1981benney'])
item(488,'P000916','claim','Two horizontal dimensions retain a Hamiltonian formulation under appropriate variables',r'The third announcement is only \ru{что-то для двумерной зависимости (с гамильтоновостью)}: something for two-dimensional dependence, with Hamiltonian structure.',r'One definite potential-flow version has horizontal \(r\in\mathbb R^2\), layer density \(\rho(r,a,t)>0\), potential \(\phi(r,a,t)\), \(u=\nabla_r\phi\), \(h=\int\rho\,da\), and \[E=\tfrac12\int\!\left(\int\rho|\nabla_r\phi|^2\,da+h^2\right)dr.\] The canonical equations \(\rho_t=\delta E/\delta\phi\), \(\phi_t=-\delta E/\delta\rho\) give \(\rho_t+\operatorname{div}_r(\rho u)=0\) and \(u_t+(u\cdot\nabla_r)u+\nabla_r h=0\). General vortical flows require additional Clebsch or noncanonical variables and their own local/topological hypotheses.',r'This is a precise identified special case of an imprecise announcement, not a claim that the notebook proposed only potential flows. It does not assert two-dimensional complete integrability.','A',r'\citet[Secs.~2 and 5]{zakharov1980benney} develops multidimensional variables; \citet[Sec.~2]{zakharov1981benney} supplies the many-fluid interpretation. The match to the compressed third announcement is partial.','A Hamiltonian structure exists in the stated variables and domain; the notes contain no definite stronger two-dimensional theorem to mark solved or open.',['zakharov1980benney','zakharov1981benney'],pm='partial',conf='medium')
item(488,'P000916','method','The many-fluid variables also give a kinetic reinterpretation',r'The general idea is to reinterpret the equation, followed immediately by Gelfand\textquotesingle s description of collective evolution through a common field.',r'For smooth layer fields satisfying \(\rho_t+\operatorname{div}_r(\rho u)=0\), \(u_t+(u\cdot\nabla_r)u=-\nabla_r h\), define the distribution in velocity \[f(r,v,t)=\int\rho(r,a,t)\,\delta(v-u(r,a,t))\,da,\qquad h=\int f\,dv.\] Then \(f_t+v\cdot\nabla_r f-\nabla_r h\cdot\nabla_v f=0\) distributionally. This gives a Vlasov-type collective-field equation. Its equivalence with a smooth hydrostatic velocity profile requires an invertible layer coordinate and, when solving for the velocity label, an appropriate monotonicity condition.',r'The distributional formula makes the identified reinterpretation explicit. In two velocity dimensions a one-parameter family of layers gives a special class of velocity distributions; it is not every Vlasov solution.','A',r'\citet[Sec.~2]{zakharov1980benney} and \citet[p.~195, Eqs.~(13)--(15)]{zakharov1981benney} state the kinetic reformulation of this Benney model.','Established for the stated smooth layer solutions, with the distribution interpreted weakly. The converse needs the additional invertibility hypotheses.',['zakharov1980benney','zakharov1981benney'],pm='partial')
item(488,'P000916','remark','Gelfand interprets the system as collective differential equations',r'Gelfand describes particles that do not interact directly, but jointly produce a field (an electric field is mentioned) which acts on every particle; he proposes studying such collective equations after ordinary differential equations.',r'A precise schematic model is a kinetic transport equation \(\partial_t f+v\cdot\nabla_xf+F[f]\cdot\nabla_vf=0\), where the common field \(F[f]\) is determined by the distribution through a separately specified field equation. The Benney reinterpretation uses \(F[f]=-\nabla_x\int f\,dv\); an electrostatic plasma uses a Poisson field instead. These are different closures of the same collective-dynamics idea.',r'The schematic equation and distinction between closures explain the analogy; no unique field equation or new well-posedness problem can be extracted from the intervention alone.','E',r'The general collective-field concept predates the talk: \citet[Secs.~1--2]{vlasov1968vibrational} is the English version of the 1938 paper, as the publisher\textquotesingle s Russian reprint record explicitly confirms. The specific Benney closure is given in \citet[Eq.~(15)]{zakharov1981benney}.','An established interpretation, not a newly stated precise conjecture. The original Vlasov publisher record and table of contents were checked; its full proof was not needed for the displayed Benney identity.',['vlasov1968vibrational','zakharov1981benney'],pm='thematic only',v='publisher metadata checked',speaker='I. M. Gelfand')
corr('P000915','Vertical Euler equation and the hydrostatic approximation',r'\(w_t+(u\cdot\nabla)w+w w_z-p_z+1=0\), with only part of the vertical acceleration bracketed for neglect.',r'The pressure term must be \(+p_z\) in the convention used for the horizontal equation; neglect the whole vertical material acceleration to obtain \(p_z+1=0\) and \(p=h-z\).','The minus sign is present in the original scan, so this is a faithful mathematical error. P000916 explicitly gives p_z+1=0 and p=h-z, fixing the intended convention.')
